APS-009 — Policy Governance

Document ID: APS-009
Title: Policy Governance
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Governance Model (CPGM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Governance Model establishes the authorities, responsibilities, governance processes, decision rights, accountability mechanisms, and oversight structures governing Canonical Policy Objects throughout their lifecycle.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Governance architecture
Governance authorities
Policy ownership
Stewardship
Review boards
Delegation
Change governance
Policy accountability

This specification applies to every Canonical Policy Object.

3. Canonical Definition

Policy Governance is the governed system of authorities, responsibilities, controls, and oversight that ensures Canonical Policy Objects are created, approved, maintained, enforced, and retired in accordance with Atlas governance principles.

Policy Governance governs policies.

It does not evaluate or enforce policies.

4. Objectives

The Policy Governance Model SHALL:

Establish accountability.
Preserve policy integrity.
Support controlled evolution.
Enable transparent decision-making.
Preserve trust.
Support federation.
Enable auditing.
Ensure policy quality.
5. Governance Principles

Every Policy Governance implementation SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Accountability

Every policy SHALL have a clearly identified owner accountable for its correctness and continued suitability.

Separation of Duties

Policy creation, review, approval, and audit SHALL be performed by distinct governance roles where organizationally feasible.

Transparency

Governance decisions SHALL be recorded and explainable.

Delegation

Governance authority MAY be delegated.

Delegated authority SHALL remain traceable to the originating authority.

Review

Every active policy SHALL undergo periodic governance review.

Auditability

Governance activities SHALL remain permanently auditable.

6. Governance Roles

Atlas defines the following canonical governance roles.

Role	Responsibility
Policy Owner	Business accountability
Policy Steward	Day-to-day policy management
Policy Author	Creates policy definitions
Policy Reviewer	Technical and governance review
Policy Approver	Grants operational approval
Governance Board	Resolves strategic governance decisions
Domain Authority	Governs policies within a governance domain
Auditor	Independent verification

Organizations MAY define additional roles without altering canonical role semantics.

7. Governance Authorities

Atlas recognizes multiple governance authorities.

Authority	Scope
Global Atlas Authority	Atlas-wide governance
Organization Authority	Organization-specific governance
Domain Authority	Engineering discipline governance
Project Authority	Project-specific governance
Regulatory Authority	External regulatory governance

Authorities SHALL define their governance boundaries explicitly.

8. Governance Domains

Policies SHALL belong to one or more Governance Domains.

Examples include:

Structural Engineering
Architecture
Transportation
Construction
Utilities
Digital Twin
Security
Compliance
AI Governance

Each Governance Domain SHALL define:

Domain owner
Applicable policies
Review cadence
Delegation rules
Compliance obligations
9. Governance Decisions

Governance SHALL authorize decisions including:

Policy creation
Policy approval
Policy publication
Policy activation
Policy suspension
Policy supersession
Policy retirement
Emergency override

Every governance decision SHALL be recorded as a Decision Record.

10. Delegation Model

Governance delegation SHALL specify:

Delegating authority
Receiving authority
Delegated scope
Effective period
Constraints
Revocation conditions

Delegation SHALL NOT transfer ultimate accountability unless explicitly authorized.

11. Change Governance

Policy changes SHALL require:

Change request
Impact assessment
Technical review
Governance review
Approval
Publication authorization

Emergency changes SHALL follow expedited but governed procedures.

12. Review Model

Policies SHALL be reviewed:

Periodically
Following significant incidents
Following regulatory changes
Following engineering standard updates
Following organizational restructuring
Upon governance request

Review outcomes SHALL be recorded.

13. Governance Lifecycle

Governance activities SHALL progress through the following lifecycle.

Requested
     │
Assigned
     │
Reviewed
     │
Approved
     │
Monitored
     │
Reassessed
     │
Closed

Lifecycle history SHALL remain immutable.

14. Governance Evidence

Every governance activity SHALL preserve:

Participants
Decisions
Supporting evidence
Review comments
Approval records
Policy versions
Audit references

Governance evidence SHALL remain immutable.

15. Governance Quality

Governance SHALL be evaluated using:

Ownership completeness
Review timeliness
Approval integrity
Delegation correctness
Audit coverage
Policy quality
Compliance performance
Decision traceability

Quality metrics SHALL themselves be governed.

16. Conformance

A Policy Governance implementation conforms if it:

Assigns canonical governance roles.
Defines governance authorities.
Implements governed change control.
Preserves governance evidence.
Supports delegation.
Maintains auditability.
Preserves accountability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted governance recommendations.
Dynamic governance domains.
Federated governance councils.
Risk-based review scheduling.
Automated governance quality assessment.
Cross-jurisdiction governance coordination.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical governance semantics.

